## Verdict

**Approved for one root-owned native execution.** The harness is bounded and
targets the already compiled `platform::gate_path::tests::exclusive_regular_creation_child`
   helper from the corrected build admission. Before spawning children it also
   reads its own cgroup `cpu.max` and requires exactly `100000 100000`. It does
   not alter Rust source or compile anything.

For each of five private fixture roots it installs a symlink, regular file,
hardlink, FIFO, or nonempty directory at `shared.bundle`, then starts two
children with private environment/cwd and null standard streams. Both children
must reach the finite readiness barrier; the parent releases them once; both
must exit successfully with `loser`, and the preinstalled entry's device,
inode, mode, link count, type, and content must remain unchanged. The canary
bytes are checked after both children settle, including the symlink target and
directory canary. This directly fills the THE-222 hostile-entry/two-child gap
identified in the maintenance07 evidence review.

The readiness, release, completion, and final child-settlement waits are
bounded. On failure, `settle` kills and reaps within a one-second cleanup
window where possible and retains the private fixture if a child remains
unsettled. Cleanup runs only after settlement and names the two child fixture
directories, release marker, target, canary, and directory canary explicitly;
it is nonrecursive. The script checks the pinned host binary SHA-256 against
the corrected build admission and checks that admission's source hash and
successful exit before launching children.

The harness is intentionally an observed preexisting-entry refusal test. It
makes no claim of an atomic lease against an arbitrary same-UID replacement
between identity observation and pathname removal. Root should record the
native receipt separately and preserve any retained fixture for investigation
if settlement or cleanup fails.

Source inspection and `python3 -m py_compile` were the only checks performed by
this review. No Cargo, Rust compiler, test binary, provider, native execution,
canonical, or Linear operation was performed.
